🧱 High-Level Architecture

The project is divided into layers, from hardware-dependent to application logic

Application (WHAT the system does)
└── Drivers (Device-specific logic)
    └── HAL (HOW the hardware is accessed)
        └── Arduino / MCU

This separation makes the code:
1-easier to maintain
2-portable to other MCUs
3-easier to test

📁 Root Files
platformio.ini
Purpose: Build and board configuration
1-Selects MCU and framework
2-Enables C compilation
3-Controls compiler flags

📁 src/ — Source Code (implementation)

This is where all .c files live.
🔹 main.c

Role: System entry point
1-Arduino calls setup() once
2-Arduino calls loop() repeatedly
3-Acts as the bridge between Arduino runtime and your application
➡ No application logic here — only system startup and scheduling.

📁 app/ — Application Layer
Purpose:
Defines system behavior and logic flow
What belongs here:
1-State machines
2-Control logic
3-High-level algorithms
4-Timing and sequencing

What does NOT belong here:
❌ GPIO
❌ Registers
❌ Arduino API calls
➡ The app asks for actions, it does not care how they are done.

📁 drivers/ — Device Drivers
Purpose:
Handle specific peripherals (LEDs, buttons, sensors, displays)
Responsibilities:
1-Encapsulate device behavior
2-Use HAL functions
3-Hide hardware details from the app
➡ The driver knows:
which pin is used
how the device behaves
➡ The application does NOT.

📁 hal/ — Hardware Abstraction Layer
Purpose:
Abstract the MCU / framework specifics
Responsibilities:
1-Wrap Arduino functions
2-Provide portable APIs

Allow easy migration to:
ESP32
STM32
Bare-metal
➡ If you change platform, only HAL changes.

📁 config/ — Configuration Layer
Purpose:
Centralize board-specific definitions
Contains:
1- Pin mappings
2-Feature switches
3-Compile-time configuration

📁 include/ — Global Headers
Purpose:
Project-wide definitions
Typical contents:
1-Common types
2-Versioning
3-Global macros

📁 lib/ — External Libraries
Purpose:
1-Third-party or reusable modules
2-PlatformIO automatically includes them
3-Keeps vendor code separate from your code

📁 test/ — Unit Tests
Purpose:
Automated testing (optional but recommended)
1-Driver tests
2-Logic tests
3-Hardware-mock tests

✅ Why This Structure Is Important
Benefit	        Explanation
Scalability	    Easy to grow to large projects
Portability	    Change MCU without rewriting logic
Testability	    Drivers and app can be tested independently
Readability	    New engineers understand faster
Professional	Matches industry practices

🧠 Mental Model
main.c → system startup
app → what the system does
drivers → how devices behave
hal → how hardware is accessed
config → what hardware exists